    "q-177715": {
      "dom_id": "step-q177715",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the $14$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the $3$rd term is $40$ and the $23$rd term is $-20.$",
      "worked_solution": "The formula for the $n$th term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\n \nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{3}=40$ and ${a}_{23}=-20$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{23}-{a}_{3}}{23-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-20-40}{23-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-60)}{20} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{3}=40,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{3}$ is $2$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}+2d \\\\ 40 & ={a}_{1}+2(-3) \\\\ 40 & ={a}_{1}-6 \\\\ 40+6 & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =46+(n-1)(-3) \\\\  & =46-3n+3 \\\\  & =49-3n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find the $14$th term by substituting $n=14$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{14} & =49-3(14) \\\\  & =49-42 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
